{
 "lead": "Amplitude modulation (AM) makes the peak value of a high-frequency carrier rise and fall in step with a low-frequency message. The message rides on the outline of the carrier, the envelope, and a simple diode circuit can read it back. One number, the modulation index m, says how deep the swing is. It sets the height of the two sidebands and the share of power that carries the message. It does not set the bandwidth, which is twice the highest message frequency. Medium-wave and shortwave broadcasting and aviation voice radio still use AM.",
 "prereq": "You need: the Fourier transform of a cosine and the idea of a band-pass signal, from the Fourier transform review and band-pass signals.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "The AM wave and the modulation index",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Take a carrier A<sub>c</sub> cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t, with peak A<sub>c</sub> in volts and frequency f<sub>c</sub> in hertz, and a tone message A<sub>m</sub> cos 2πf<sub>m</sub>t, with peak A<sub>m</sub> and frequency f<sub>m</sub> much smaller than f<sub>c</sub>. Conventional AM adds the message to the carrier's amplitude:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>s(t) = A<sub>c</sub>[1 + m cos 2πf<sub>m</sub>t] cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "s(t) is the AM voltage in volts at time t in seconds; m = A<sub>m</sub>/A<sub>c</sub> is the modulation index, a pure number. Percentage modulation is 100m %."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The bracket A<sub>c</sub>[1 + m cos 2πf<sub>m</sub>t] is the envelope. It swings between V<sub>max</sub> = A<sub>c</sub>(1 + m) and V<sub>min</sub> = A<sub>c</sub>(1 − m). An oscilloscope shows both, so m is usually measured from them:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>m = (V<sub>max</sub> − V<sub>min</sub>)/(V<sub>max</sub> + V<sub>min</sub>)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "V<sub>max</sub> and V<sub>min</sub> are the largest and smallest envelope peaks in volts; half their sum is A<sub>c</sub> and half their difference is A<sub>m</sub>."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "If m > 1 (overmodulation), 1 + m cos 2πf<sub>m</sub>t goes negative for part of each cycle. The carrier flips phase, the envelope loses the shape of the message, an envelope detector distorts, and a real transmitter clips and sprays extra sidebands into neighbouring channels. So AM keeps m ≤ 1, with only the loudest peaks near 1."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Line spectrum and bandwidth",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Expanding with 2 cos A cos B = cos(A − B) + cos(A + B) gives three cosines:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>s(t) = A<sub>c</sub> cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t + (mA<sub>c</sub>/2) cos 2π(f<sub>c</sub> + f<sub>m</sub>)t + (mA<sub>c</sub>/2) cos 2π(f<sub>c</sub> − f<sub>m</sub>)t</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "These are the carrier at f<sub>c</sub> with amplitude A<sub>c</sub>, the upper sideband (USB) at f<sub>c</sub> + f<sub>m</sub> and the lower sideband (LSB) at f<sub>c</sub> − f<sub>m</sub>, each of amplitude mA<sub>c</sub>/2. The sidebands carry the message: their distance from the carrier is the message frequency and their height follows the message amplitude. The carrier line is the same with or without a message. The figure draws the envelope and these three lines for the first worked example."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>B<sub>AM</sub> = 2f<sub>m</sub>   (a band of tones: 2W)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "B<sub>AM</sub> is the bandwidth in hertz and W the highest message frequency. Medium-wave stations in India sit 9 kHz apart, room for about 4.5 kHz of audio."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A larger m makes the sidebands taller but does not move them, so the bandwidth stays 2f<sub>m</sub>."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Power, efficiency, antenna current and several tones",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A cosine of peak V across a resistance R delivers V²/(2R). Applied to each line:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>P<sub>c</sub> = A<sub>c</sub>²/(2R),  P<sub>USB</sub> = P<sub>LSB</sub> = m²P<sub>c</sub>/4</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "P<sub>c</sub> is the carrier power in watts and R the load (antenna) resistance in ohms."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "P<sub>t</sub> = P<sub>c</sub>(1 + m²/2)",
     "where": "P<sub>t</sub> is the total power: the carrier plus two sidebands of m²P<sub>c</sub>/4 each."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The transmission efficiency η is the share of power in the sidebands: η = (m²P<sub>c</sub>/2)/P<sub>t</sub> = m²/(2 + m²). At m = 1, η = 1/3 = 33.3 %, the best tone-modulated AM can do; at m = 0.5 it is 11.1 %. The wasted carrier buys a cheap receiver."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Power in a fixed antenna resistance goes as the square of the rms antenna current, so I<sub>t</sub> = I<sub>c</sub>√(1 + m²/2), where I<sub>c</sub> is the current with the carrier alone and I<sub>t</sub> the modulated current. An RF ammeter therefore measures m: m = √(2[(I<sub>t</sub>/I<sub>c</sub>)² − 1])."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "With several tones of indices m<sub>1</sub>, m<sub>2</sub>, … the sideband powers add, so the total index is m<sub>t</sub> = √(m<sub>1</sub>² + m<sub>2</sub>² + …) and P<sub>t</sub> = P<sub>c</sub>(1 + m<sub>t</sub>²/2). The envelope stays positive even when all tones peak together only if m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> + … ≤ 1."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "An AM wave on a 1 MHz carrier has an envelope that peaks at V<sub>max</sub> = 12 V and dips to V<sub>min</sub> = 4 V. The tone is 5 kHz and the load is 50 Ω. Find m, A<sub>c</sub>, A<sub>m</sub>, the sidebands, the bandwidth, the powers and the efficiency.",
   "steps": [
    "m = (12 − 4)/(12 + 4) = 8/16 = 0.5 (50 %).",
    "A<sub>c</sub> = (12 + 4)/2 = 8 V and A<sub>m</sub> = (12 − 4)/2 = 4 V; check A<sub>m</sub>/A<sub>c</sub> = 0.5.",
    "USB at 1000 + 5 = 1005 kHz, LSB at 995 kHz, each mA<sub>c</sub>/2 = 0.5 × 8/2 = 2 V.",
    "B = 2f<sub>m</sub> = 10 kHz, from 995 kHz to 1005 kHz.",
    "P<sub>c</sub> = 8²/(2 × 50) = 0.64 W. Each sideband: 2²/(2 × 50) = 0.04 W, which equals m²P<sub>c</sub>/4 = 0.25 × 0.64/4.",
    "P<sub>t</sub> = 0.64 × (1 + 0.25/2) = 0.72 W = 0.64 + 2 × 0.04 W.",
    "η = 0.25/2.25 = 11.1 %; check 0.08/0.72 = 0.111."
   ],
   "answer": "m = 0.5, A<sub>c</sub> = 8 V, A<sub>m</sub> = 4 V; 2 V sidebands at 995 and 1005 kHz; B = 10 kHz; P<sub>c</sub> = 0.64 W, 0.04 W per sideband, P<sub>t</sub> = 0.72 W; η = 11.1 %."
  },
  {
   "question": "An antenna current is 10 A with the carrier alone and 10.8 A when a tone modulates it; the antenna resistance is 50 Ω. Find m. A second tone of index 0.4 is added: find the total index, the antenna current and the total power.",
   "steps": [
    "(10.8/10)² = 1.1664, so m<sub>1</sub>² = 2 × 0.1664 = 0.3328 and m<sub>1</sub> = 0.577.",
    "P<sub>c</sub> = I<sub>c</sub>²R = 10² × 50 = 5000 W (rms current, so no factor ½).",
    "m<sub>t</sub>² = 0.3328 + 0.4² = 0.4928, m<sub>t</sub> = 0.702.",
    "I<sub>t</sub> = 10√(1 + 0.4928/2) = 10√1.2464 = 11.16 A.",
    "P<sub>t</sub> = 5000 × 1.2464 = 6232 W; check 11.164² × 50 = 6232 W. Peak check: 0.577 + 0.4 = 0.977 ≤ 1."
   ],
   "answer": "m<sub>1</sub> = 0.577; m<sub>t</sub> = 0.702, I<sub>t</sub> = 11.16 A, P<sub>t</sub> = 6232 W."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Using peak-to-peak readings as peaks. An envelope of 24 V and 8 V peak-to-peak means V<sub>max</sub> = 12 V and V<sub>min</sub> = 4 V; m is still 0.5, but A<sub>c</sub> is 8 V, not 16 V.",
  "Writing the sideband amplitude as mA<sub>c</sub>. It is mA<sub>c</sub>/2: 2 V, not 4 V, in the first example.",
  "Adding indices for several tones. Indices 0.577 and 0.4 give m<sub>t</sub> = 0.702, not 0.977.",
  "Using I²R/2 with an ammeter reading. Ammeters read rms, so 10 A in 50 Ω is 5000 W, not 2500 W."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Write the tone-modulated AM wave and define the modulation index.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "s(t) = A<sub>c</sub>[1 + m cos 2πf<sub>m</sub>t] cos 2πf<sub>c</sub>t, with m = A<sub>m</sub>/A<sub>c</sub>, the ratio of message peak to carrier peak; it has no unit."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does the AM bandwidth not depend on m?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "The sidebands sit at f<sub>c</sub> ± f<sub>m</sub> for every m; m only sets their height mA<sub>c</sub>/2. So the band is 2f<sub>m</sub> wide for any m ≤ 1."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "An envelope varies between 15 V and 5 V. Find m and A<sub>c</sub>.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "m = (15 − 5)/(15 + 5) = 0.5; A<sub>c</sub> = (15 + 5)/2 = 10 V."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A transmitter radiates 10 kW at m = 0.6. Find the carrier power and the power in each sideband.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "P<sub>c</sub> = 10/1.18 = 8.475 kW; both sidebands 10 − 8.475 = 1.525 kW, so 0.763 kW each."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Three tones have indices 0.3, 0.4 and 0.5. Find the total index.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "m<sub>t</sub> = √(0.09 + 0.16 + 0.25) = √0.5 = 0.707."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A student puts m = 1.5 into P<sub>t</sub> = P<sub>c</sub>(1 + m²/2) and reports 2.125P<sub>c</sub>. Why is this misleading?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "At m = 1.5 the envelope goes negative and a real transmitter clips, adding sidebands at multiples of f<sub>m</sub>. The three-line spectrum behind the formula no longer exists, so the number does not describe the real signal."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Show that tone-modulated AM never puts more than a third of its power in the sidebands.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "η = m²/(2 + m²) rises with m; the largest usable m is 1, giving η = 1/3 = 33.3 %."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Many AM broadcast transmitters now vary the carrier level with the programme (dynamic carrier control) to save electricity. Digital Radio Mondiale (DRM) sends digital audio in the same AM channels, and India's medium-wave network runs DRM transmitters. Search next for dynamic carrier control and DRM30.",
